# Android upload signing. A committed keystore is an unrecoverable mistake:
# Play binds an app to its upload key, and a leaked one lets anyone publish.
*.jks
*.keystore
mobile/android/thoughtpins-app/keystore.properties

# macOS and Xcode local state. None of this describes the project: .DS_Store is
# Finder's, .build is SwiftPM's cache, and xcuserdata/project.xcworkspace hold
# one developer's window and scheme state, which Xcode rewrites on open.
.DS_Store
.build/
# `**/` matters: a pattern with a slash in the middle is anchored to this file's
# directory, so the previous `*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/` only ever matched a
# project at the repository root. It never covered the real one under
# mobile/ios/ThoughtPinsNative/, which was ignored wholesale instead. Now that
# the project itself is tracked, these have to match where it actually lives.
**/*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/
**/*.xcodeproj/project.xcworkspace/

# ThoughtPins.xcodeproj IS committed, deliberately. `project.yml` remains the
# source of truth and `ci_scripts/ci_post_clone.sh` regenerates the project on
# every cloud build, so nothing is built from the committed copy -- it exists
# because App Store Connect's web UI scans the repository at workflow-creation
# time and refuses to create a workflow when it finds no shared scheme. The
# generation step inside the build is too late for that scan.
#
# The tradeoff, stated so the next person knows it was chosen: this file was
# ignored because two machines generating it can produce conflicting pbxproj
# diffs. Measured at the pinned XcodeGen 2.46.0 the output is byte-identical
# across regenerations and across checkout directories, so the conflict does
# not arise while everyone uses the pin -- a different XcodeGen version is what
# would reintroduce it. Weighed against that: this is the only Mac, it cannot
# produce an App Store archive at all, and leaving the project out means every
# future release needs someone sitting at it. Releases from Windows are worth
# more than a diff that the pin already prevents.
#
# If it ever does churn, regenerate with the pinned version and commit the
# result; do not build from it, and do not resolve a conflict by hand.
#
# There is no rule here for it: the entry that ignored it was removed, and the
# two `*.xcodeproj/` rules above still exclude the per-developer subdirectories
# inside it. A `!` negation would be wrong -- it would come after those rules
# and re-include xcuserdata and project.xcworkspace with them.
# Opening a Swift package in Xcode writes per-developer scheme state here. It
# appeared the first time anyone opened the project in the GUI rather than
# building it from the command line.
.swiftpm/
/Thought Pins/
